<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Steponas Darius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Steponas Darius was a Lithuanian-American aviator who with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stasys-girenas">Stasys Girėnas</a> attempted a non-stop flight from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> in the aircraft Lituanica in July 1933. After crossing the Atlantic in about 37 hours, the plane crashed near Soldin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, killing both men short of their goal. Their flight became a national legend, and the two airmen appeared on the Lithuanian ten-litas banknote.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/steponas-darius.jpg" alt="Steponas Darius" width="305" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S.Darius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Steponas Jucevičius-Darašius on 8 January 1896 in Rubiškės, in the Kovno Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> with his family in 1907. In 1917, after the United States entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, he joined the United States Army and changed his name to Darius. He served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> operator in the 149th Field Artillery Regiment, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was wounded and received the Purple Heart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1920 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and joined the Lithuanian Army, graduating from the War School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> in 1921, and he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda-revolt">Klaipėda Revolt</a> of 1923. While in Lithuania he completed pilot training and promoted many sports. He played for the Lithuania national football team in its first competitive game, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> on 23 June 1923, and was in the squad for the 1924 Summer Olympics without playing a match.</p><p>He initiated the building of the first stadium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, later named the S. Darius and S. Girėnas Stadium. As the first to publish booklets on basketball and baseball, he is considered to have brought both sports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>; he was also the first chairman of the Lithuanian Physical Education Union and a founder of Sporto Žurnalas. In 1927 he returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to work in civil aviation, forming South Bend Airways with Carl G. Jordan of South Bend, Indiana, before moving to Chicago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Darius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stasys-girenas">Stasys Girėnas</a> left New York City on 15 July 1933 in a Bellanca CH-300 Pacemaker, aiming to cover 7,186 kilometres without landing. They crashed at 0:36 am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> time on 17 July by the village of Kuhdamm, near Soldin (now Pszczelnik, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>), most probably because of bad weather combined with engine problems. They had flown 6,411 kilometres and were 650 kilometres short of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>.</p><p>On 18 May 1934 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> issued six airmail stamps in honour of the two captains and the Lituanica. A monument to them stands in Marquette Park in Chicago, a World War II Liberty ship was named SS Stepas Darius, his birth village was renamed Darius, and the asteroid 288960 Steponasdarius, discovered in 2004, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Steponas Darius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The trans-Atlantic flight of <b>Steponas Darius</b> and Stasys Girėnas in 1933, which ended in a fatal crash in Germany, became a national legend.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Darius, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in Myślibórz, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.6,21.85&amp;year=1920" aria-label="Darius on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5027 267 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Steponas Darius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5477 537h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5477 537h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.6,21.85&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
